WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.749
Keeping a judge calibrated.

2
00:00:02.029 --> 00:00:09.240
Calibration is a measurement at one point in time, of one judge configuration, on one distribution of outputs.

3
00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:11.995
Change any of those and the numbers can move.

4
00:00:12.275 --> 00:00:26.525
Re-run the test split whenever you change the judge’s prompt, model, or effort setting, and whenever the system being judged changes substantially, because a new generator produces new kinds of mistakes the judge may not recognize.

5
00:00:26.805 --> 00:00:40.971
Bavaresco and colleagues (2024) found that judge reliability varied with whether the text being judged was written by people or generated by models, a reminder that the distribution of inputs matters.

6
00:00:41.251 --> 00:00:50.531
Between those events, audit: each week or release, have the expert label a small random sample of production outputs the judge has already graded.

7
00:00:50.811 --> 00:00:57.744
That sample estimates current TPR and TNR on live traffic and catches drift that a frozen test set can’t.

8
00:00:58.024 --> 00:01:03.827
When the audit disagrees with the test-split numbers, believe the audit, investigate, and re-calibrate.

9
00:01:04.107 --> 00:01:07.051
The same procedure is how you choose a judge model.

10
00:01:07.331 --> 00:01:24.739
Calibrate each candidate (a small, fast model; a frontier model; a model from another provider, especially when the outputs come from Claude; an open-weight model you host) on the same labeled splits and compare TPR, TNR, kappa, and cost per thousand verdicts.

11
00:01:25.019 --> 00:01:31.632
Pick the cheapest one that meets the bar your use requires, and write down the numbers so the next person can see why.

12
00:01:31.912 --> 00:01:43.901
In the lab you’ll do all of this on 40 labeled support replies: split, write the metrics, build the judge with structured outputs, iterate on dev, report on test once, and correct a pass rate.
